{"packet_id":"D006-01","doc_id":"D006","jurisdiction":"Berkeley, CA","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Measure BB cap on the Annual General Adjustment","requirement":"The Rent Board may not approve an Annual General Adjustment above 5% in any year; landlords with banked prior-year AGAs may still raise rent to the ceiling. Rent increase petitions are allowed only for completed capital improvements.","key_value":"AGA max 5% per year","coverage_conditions":"Fully covered units (most multifamily units built before 1980) and partially covered units, including new construction with a certificate of occupancy after 1980, most single-family homes and condominiums, and government-owned or -subsidized units.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":"1980","built_after":"1980","date_basis":"certificate_of_occupancy","min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Government-owned or -subsidized units now fully or partially covered; shared kitchen or bath units exempt only if the landlord lived on the same property at the start of the tenancy"},"exemptions":"Units where the tenant shares a kitchen or bath with the landlord are exempt only if the landlord lived on the same property at the start of the tenancy; most single-family homes and condominiums are only partially covered.","penalty":"The Rent Board may adopt fines for noncompliance with registration requirements, including failure to timely report tenancy information.","effective_date":null,"citation":"Berkeley Mun. Code ch. 13.76","quoted_span":"The maximum AGA is 5%. This means that, if the formula in the Rent Ordinance results in an AGA that is greater than 5%, the AGA approved by the Board for that year can be no more than 5%.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D006-01","doc_id":"D006","jurisdiction":"Berkeley, CA","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Measure BB just cause eviction and notice changes","requirement":"Covered tenants may be evicted only for just cause. Nonpayment eviction requires rental debt of at least one month's Fair Market Rent; failure to sign a substantially similar lease is no longer cause; lease-breach evictions require actual harm. The Rent Board must adjust relocation payments yearly.","key_value":"1 month's Fair Market Rent nonpayment threshold","coverage_conditions":"Tenants in units that are fully or partially covered by the Berkeley Rent Ordinance.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":"1980","built_after":"1980","date_basis":"certificate_of_occupancy","min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Government-subsidized units are partially covered regardless of unit type where federal or state law prohibits rent control"},"exemptions":"Units exempt from the Rent Ordinance; government-owned or -subsidized units such as Section 202 and Section 811 housing are partially covered regardless of unit type.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"Berkeley Mun. Code ch. 13.76","quoted_span":"For a landlord to evict a tenant for nonpayment of rent, the tenant must owe an amount of rental debt equal to or greater than one month of the Fair Market Rent (FMR) value for a unit of equivalent size in the metro area Oakland-Fremont, CA HUD Metro FMR as determined by the US Department of Housing and Urban Development for the fiscal year in which the rent is demanded.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
